<p>J.A. Lindon composed this remarkable pair of monosyllabic poems in 1969. The two read the same, but each has its own rhyme scheme, and each is a distinct acrostic, the first letter of each line spelling out the title.</p>
<p><em>Cats, Cats, Cats, Cats!</em></p>
<p>Cats come with mews,<br />
All have sharp claws,<br />
They air their views.<br />
Stray soon from laws,<br />
Climb wall. Each tom<br />
And each mog &#8212; grand<br />
Thieves &#8212; munch meal from<br />
Scamp&#8217;s lunch. Scamp planned &#8230;</p>
<p>Chain off, he might<br />
Aim at a rout,<br />
Touch mogs and bite<br />
Such prime bits out!<br />
Chance came. Grey dawn.<br />
At once left mat.<br />
The deuce! On lawn -­<br />
Scamp loose &#8212; no cat!</p>
<p><em>Chase &#8217;em, Scamp, Go On!</em></p>
<p>Cats come with mews, all<br />
Have sharp claws, they<br />
Air their views, stray<br />
Soon from laws, climb wall.</p>
<p>Each tom and<br />
Each mog &#8212; grand thieves &#8212; munch<br />
Meal from Scamp&#8217;s lunch.<br />
Scamp planned &#8230;</p>
<p>Chain off, he might aim<br />
At a rout, touch<br />
Mogs and bite such<br />
Prime bits out. Chance came.</p>
<p>Grey dawn. At<br />
Once left mat. The deuce!<br />
On lawn &#8212; Scamp loose -­<br />
No cat!</p>
